.he "pr (1)                       8/16/26                       pr (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
pr
- print file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
pr [ option ] ... [ file ] ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Pr produces a printed listing of one or more files.  The output is
split into pages headed by the date, the name of the file or a header
given with -h, and the page number.  With no file, pr reads its
standard input.
.sp
Options apply to all the files after them and may be reset between
files.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-n	Produce n columns, as in -3.

.ti -10
+n	Begin printing with page n.

.ti -10
-h header	Take the next argument as the page header.

.ti -10
-wn	Take the width of the page as n characters instead of the default 72.

.ti -10
-ln	Take the length of the page as n lines instead of the default 66.

.ti -10
-f	Use formfeeds rather than blank lines to separate pages.

.ti -10
-t	Print no page header and no trailer.

.ti -10
-sc	Separate columns by the single character c; a bare -s means a tab.

.ti -10
-m	Print all the files at once, each in its own column.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Pr no longer blocks write (1) messages to the terminal while it
prints.  The original did it by chmod'ing the tty and putting the
mode back on the way out, which wanted ttyname and this libc has
none.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cat (1)
